\id LUK - Aperto Bible (English) v4.3
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Luke
\toc1 The Gospel According to Luke
\toc2 Luke
\toc3 Lk
\mt1 Luke

\c 2

\s1 The Birth of Jesus

\p
\v 1 In those days, a decree went out from Caesar Augustus\f + \fr 2:1 \fk NAME \fq Augustus \ft The title “Augustus” (Latin) or Sebastos (Σεβαστός) in Greek means “the Exalted One” or “the Revered.” Official Roman inscriptions hailed him as “savior of the world” and “lord.” Luke frames Jesus's birth inside the machinery of the world's most powerful empire — which will unknowingly be used to fulfill a prophecy about the city of David.\f* — a registration of the whole Roman world\f + \fr 2:1 \fk KONTEXT \fq whole Roman world (οἰκουμένη) \ft The Greek oikoumenē referred to the Roman-ruled inhabited world. Luke deliberately places the birth announcement within the scope of Caesar's power — while the truly world-changing event happens in a village no census planner would notice.\f* was to be taken.
\v 2 This was the first registration, while Quirinius governed Syria.\f + \fr 2:2 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq Quirinius \ft The chronology here is the most discussed historical difficulty in Luke's Gospel. According to the historian Josephus, Quirinius became governor of Syria in 6 CE, while Herod — under whom Matthew places the birth — died in 4 BCE. This creates a gap of at least ten years. Proposed solutions include an earlier command of Quirinius in the region, a translation of πρώτη as “before” rather than “first,” and the possibility that Luke compresses or connects two separate registrations. The historical tensions are real, and honest scholarship acknowledges them. For a fuller treatment, see the sidebar “The Quirinius Problem.” Luke's primary claim is theological: God acts in real, datable history — not myth or legend — even when we cannot resolve every chronological detail.\f*
\v 3 And everyone went to register, each to his own town.
\p
\v 4 Joseph also went up from Galilee, from the town of Nazareth, into Judea — to the city of David, called Bethlehem,\f + \fr 2:4 \fk NAME \fq Bethlehem \ft The Hebrew בֵּית לֶחֶם (Beit Lehem) means “house of bread.” The prophet Micah had said the ruler of Israel would come from Bethlehem (Micah 5:2). It was the city associated with David — and now the one born “Son of David” is born there. Ancient readers would have heard “house of bread” as the place where the Bread of Life enters the world.\f* because he was of the house and lineage of David.
\v 5 He went to register with Mary, his betrothed, who was pregnant. \add The journey was several days, mostly uphill.\add*
\v 6 While they were there, the time came.
\v 7 She gave birth to her firstborn son, wrapped him in cloths, and laid him in a feeding trough.\f + \fr 2:7 \fk TEXT \fq guest room (κατάλυμα) \ft The Greek katalyma refers to a guest room in a private home — not a commercial inn. Luke uses this same word for the “upper room” of the Last Supper (22:11). The word for a commercial inn is pandocheion (πανδοχεῖον), which Luke uses in 10:34. First-century Palestinian homes typically had a main room, a guest space, and a lower-level area where animals sheltered at night — with a stone feeding trough (φάτνη) built into the floor. There is no innkeeper in this story; the family guest room was simply already full.\f* There was no room for them in the guest room.

\s1 The Shepherds and the Angels

\p
\v 8 There were shepherds out in the fields that night, keeping watch over their flocks.\f + \fr 2:8 \fk KULTUR \fq shepherds \ft First-century shepherds occupied a low social position in Palestinian society. The Mishnah classified them as generally unreliable witnesses in court (m. Sanhedrin 3:3). Their work — constant movement, night shifts in the fields, direct contact with animals — kept them from observing Sabbath and purity regulations. Cultural anthropologists (Bruce Malina, Jerome Neyrey) describe this honor-shame society: shepherds lived at the margins of what “respectable” people considered reliable. They were economically necessary but socially suspect. That God chose them as the first recipients of the most important announcement in history follows Luke's consistent pattern: the economically poor, the socially marginal, and the ritually excluded are the ones God approaches first.\f* \add Men who slept under the sky and were counted for little — their testimony not trusted in court, their lives lived at the edge of what respectable society considered reliable.\add*
\v 9 \add An ordinary night.\add* And then the angel of the Lord stood before them — and the glory of the Lord blazed around them.\f + \fr 2:9 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq the glory of the Lord blazed \ft Modern readers often find supernatural accounts like this difficult to engage. The text assumes a worldview in which God communicates through messengers and in which divine presence takes visible form. The Hebrew concept kabod (כָּבוֹד, “glory,” “weight”) referred to the manifest presence of God — the same blazing brightness that filled the tabernacle in the wilderness (Exodus 40:34-35). That light appearing outdoors, in a field, at night, to shepherds: the ancient listener would have recognized immediately that the normal order was being interrupted. Interpretations of such accounts vary: some read them as visionary or symbolic experiences; the Christian tradition takes them as real events involving genuine divine presence. How readers interpret this depends on their prior commitments about God's relationship to the world.\f* \add That light was not meant to be outdoors. They knew it.\add* Great fear seized them.
\p
\v 10 “Don't be afraid.” \add The angel said it quickly. But the voice shifted — dropped into something older, heavier.\add* “I bring you news of great joy for all the people:\f + \fr 2:10 \fk KONTEXT \fq good news (εὐαγγελίζομαι) \ft The Greek euangelizomai was standard vocabulary in Roman imperial propaganda — used to announce the emperor's birth, military victories, and decrees. Inscriptions across the empire called such announcements “good news.” The angel borrows the empire's own PR vocabulary and redirects it: the truly world-changing announcement is not from the palace in Rome but from a field outside a village in occupied Judea.\f*
\v 11 today, in the city of David, a Savior\f + \fr 2:11 \fk KONTEXT \fq Savior, the Messiah, the Lord (σωτήρ, χριστὸς κύριος) \ft All three titles carried counter-imperial force. “Savior” (sōtēr, σωτήρ) was one of Augustus's official epithets, inscribed on monuments across the empire. “Lord” (kyrios, κύριος) was used both for the emperor and for the God of Israel. The combination “the Messiah, the Lord” (christos kyrios, χριστὸς κύριος) is unique in the New Testament — this precise phrase appears nowhere else. The angel is not simply announcing a birth; the announcement is a rival imperial claim, made to shepherds, about a baby in a feeding trough.\f* has been born. He is the Messiah, the Lord.
\v 12 And this is your sign — you will find him wrapped in cloths, lying in a feeding trough.”
\p
\v 13 And suddenly, with the angel, a vast army of heaven,\f + \fr 2:13 \fk TEXT \fq vast army of heaven (στρατιά) \ft The Greek stratia is a military term — an army, not a choir. “Heavenly host” preserves older English usage in which “host” once meant “army,” but modern readers hear “host” as “multitude” or “gathering.” The military connotation is deliberate: the Pax Romana was enforced by Rome's legions. Luke answers with God's army — not armed, but praising.\f* praising God:
\q1 \v 14 “Glory to God in the highest —
\q2 and on earth, peace,
\q2 among those on whom his favor rests.”\f + \fr 2:14 \fk TEXT \fq his favor rests (εὐδοκίας) \ft The Greek eudokias appears as a genitive in the oldest manuscripts (P75, Codex Sinaiticus, Codex Vaticanus), meaning “people of God's good pleasure” or “among those on whom his favor rests.” Later manuscripts have the nominative form, producing the KJV's “goodwill toward men.” Textual scholars (Bruce Metzger, Nestle-Aland) regard the genitive reading as original. The peace announced at Jesus's birth is not universal — it comes to those on whom God's favor rests, defined by the whole Gospel as the poor, the outcast, and those who receive the child.\f*
\p
\v 15 When the angels had gone back into heaven, the shepherds turned to one another. “Let's go to Bethlehem and see this thing that has happened — that the Lord has made known to us.”
\v 16 They went with haste and found Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the feeding trough.
\v 17 When they had seen it, they went and told what they had heard about this child.
\v 18 Everyone who heard it was amazed.
\p
\v 19 Mary kept all these things, turning them over in her heart.\f + \fr 2:19 \fk LEBEN \fq kept... turning them over in her heart \ft Luke uses two active verbs: syneterein (συνετήρει, “she kept/guarded”) and symballein (συμβάλλουσα, “pondering/turning over”). Mary does not simply remember — she guards and processes. Christian traditions have found in this response a model for engaging experiences that outpace understanding. Teresa of Ávila described a similar practice of “recollection” — holding difficult or holy things before God without forcing premature resolution. Things can be kept before they are explained.\f*
\p
\v 20 The shepherds returned, praising and glorifying God for everything they had heard and seen — exactly as they had been told.

\p
\v 21 On the eighth day \add — as the Torah prescribed —\add* the child was circumcised \add and enrolled in the covenant of his people.\add* His name was given: Jesus\f + \fr 2:21 \fk NAME \fq Jesus (Yeshua) \ft The Aramaic/Hebrew name Yeshua (ישׁוּעַ) means “YHWH saves.” The Greek form Iesous (Ἰησοῦς) is a transliteration of Yeshua. The name had been given by the angel before his conception (1:31). Naming and circumcision on the eighth day — the child receiving both his identity in the covenant and his personal name in a single act. His name is his mission statement.\f*. The name the angel had spoken before he was even conceived.

\s1 The Presentation in the Temple

\p
\v 22 When the days of their purification were completed \add — forty days —\add*\f + \fr 2:22 \fk KULTUR \fq their purification \ft Leviticus 12 prescribed a forty-day purification period for the mother after the birth of a male child, ending with a sacrifice at the Temple. Luke's “their” (αὐτῶν) extends the obligation to the family group. The firstborn son's dedication was a separate requirement (Exod 13:2, 12–13): every firstborn male “belongs to YHWH” — a law rooted in the story of the Exodus, when the firstborn of Israel was spared.\f* as the law of Moses required, Joseph and Mary brought the child up to Jerusalem to present him to the Lord —
\v 23 as it is written in the law of the Lord: every firstborn male is to be consecrated to the Lord —
\v 24 and to offer \add — the sacrifice permitted for those who could not afford a lamb:\add*\f + \fr 2:24 \fk KULTUR \fq turtledoves (poverty offering) \ft Leviticus 12:8 specified a lamb as the primary offering after childbirth, with two turtledoves or two young pigeons as the alternative “if she cannot afford a lamb.” The text does not editorialize; it simply records what Mary and Joseph brought. The family presenting the Savior of the world at the Temple brought the offering of the poor.\f* a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons.
\p
\v 25 In Jerusalem there was a man named Simeon\f + \fr 2:25 \fk NAME \fq Simeon (שִׁמְעוֹן) \ft The Hebrew Shim'on (שִׁמְעוֹן) means “the one who has been heard” or “God has heard.” His name is his biography: a man whose prayer God heard, who had been promised he would not die before the answer came.\f* — righteous and devout, a man the Holy Spirit rested on. \add He had organized his whole life around a single hope:\add* the consolation of Israel, \add the comfort God had promised through the prophets\add*\f + \fr 2:25 \fk KONTEXT \fq consolation of Israel (παράκλησιν) \ft The Greek paraklesis (“consolation, comfort”) echoes Isaiah 40:1 — “Comfort, comfort my people” — and the prophetic tradition of God's promised restoration after exile. The consolation of Israel referred to the messianic hope: God would return to his people, end the exile of spirit that continued even after physical return from Babylon, and restore the kingdom. Simeon was among those still actively waiting for this. The same Greek root (parakletos) would later be used for the Holy Spirit as Comforter/Advocate.\f*.
\v 26 He had been told by the Holy Spirit that he would not die before he had seen the Lord's Messiah.\f + \fr 2:26 \fk UNGLAUBLICH \fq told by the Holy Spirit \ft The text presents Simeon as having received a direct communication from the Spirit — a form of prophetic revelation. For secular readers this raises questions: how does a person “receive” a message from the Spirit? What does that actually mean or feel like? The text does not explain; it simply reports what Simeon had been told. Christianity has historically believed in the activity of the Holy Spirit in communicating with people through Scripture, inner conviction, and prophetic experience. Simeon's knowing is not based on a vision or a voice in the air — it reads as settled conviction. How one categorizes that depends on prior commitments about God's engagement with the world.\f*
\v 27 When the Spirit brought him to the Temple courts that day, he went.
\p
\v 28 Joseph and Mary brought Jesus in. And Simeon took the child in his arms. \add He had waited for this. He had waited for decades. And now he held it — and spoke:\add*
\q1 \v 29 “Now, Lord, you may let your servant go in peace,\f + \fr 2:29 \fk KONTEXT \fq Nunc Dimittis \ft The opening Latin words “Nunc dimittis” (“Now let depart”) became the traditional name for this canticle. It has been sung at Evening Prayer (Vespers) and Night Prayer (Compline) in the Christian church for over 1,500 years — in Orthodox, Catholic, Anglican, and Lutheran liturgies. Simeon's words are not resignation or despair: the Greek ἀπολύεις carries the sense of releasing a servant after completed duty. A lifetime's vigil is ending not in disappointment but in fulfillment.\f*
\q2 just as you promised —
\q1 \v 30 my eyes have seen your salvation,
\q1 \v 31 which you prepared for all peoples:
\q1 \v 32 a light for revelation to the Gentiles,
\q2 and glory for your people Israel.”
\p
\v 33 The parents stood there, amazed at what was being said about their son.
\p
\v 34 Simeon blessed them. \add He stood with them for a moment — this young couple, this child. Then he turned to Mary. Something in his face changed.\add*
\p “This child is set here for the fall of some and the rising of others in Israel,\f + \fr 2:34 \fk KONTEXT \fq fall and rising \ft Not a chronological sequence — some fall, then others rise. Rather, the same child becomes the occasion for both simultaneously, depending on the response. He is a point of crisis and division, not of predictable progress. Isaiah 8:14-15 speaks of God himself becoming “a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense.” Simeon applies that to the child. This is one of the earliest notes in Luke's Gospel that Jesus will not simply be welcomed — he will divide.\f* and for a sign that will be contradicted —
\v 35 and you, Mary — a sword will pierce through your own soul\f + \fr 2:35 \fk KONTEXT \fq a sword (ῥομφαία) \ft The ῥομφαία was a large, heavy sword — heavier than the standard short sword (machaira). The image is deliberately violent and personal. Interpretations have included: Mary's anguish watching her son die at the cross; the division Jesus will cause that will cut through Mary's own loyalties and certainties; and the ongoing pain of a mother who does not fully understand her son (cf. v. 50). Many readers find all three dimensions present. Simeon does not explain the sword; he simply announces it. The shadow falls across the scene without resolving.\f* — so that the hidden thoughts of many hearts may be uncovered.”
\p
\v 36 There was also a woman named Anna\f + \fr 2:36 \fk NAME \fq Anna (Phanuel) \ft The Hebrew חַנָּה (Hannah) means “grace” or “favor.” The same name as the Hannah of 1 Samuel — another woman who came to the Temple with a need God would answer. Carol Meyers (*Women in Scripture*) notes that Anna functions as a professional prophet with public religious authority, not merely a pious elderly woman. Her father's name Phanuel (פְּנוּאֵל) means “face of God” — the same name as the place where Jacob wrestled with God (Gen 32:30). His daughter now sees God face to face in the child.\f* — a prophetess, daughter of Phanuel, from the tribe of Asher. She was well into her years: she had been widowed young,
\v 37 and had spent her life at the Temple since then, fasting and praying, day and night.
\v 38 She came up at that very hour, gave thanks to God, and spoke about the child to everyone in Jerusalem who was waiting for the redemption of the city.\f + \fr 2:38 \fk KONTEXT \fq redemption of Jerusalem (λύτρωσιν) \ft The Greek lytrōsin (“redemption,” “release”) carried both political and theological weight: freedom from captivity, release from bondage, the reversal of exile and domination. Anna's audience — people waiting for Jerusalem's redemption — understood this in terms of liberation from Roman occupation and the restoration of God's people. Luke does not resolve the tension between political and spiritual redemption; both dimensions are present throughout his Gospel.\f*
\p
\v 39 When they had done everything the law required, they returned to Galilee, to their own town of Nazareth.
\v 40 The child grew and became strong, filled with wisdom. And the grace of God was on him.

\s1 The Boy Jesus at the Temple

\p
\v 41 Every year, Joseph and Mary went to Jerusalem for the Passover festival.\f + \fr 2:41 \fk KULTUR \fq Passover festival (Passover pilgrimage) \ft Adult Jewish males were required by Torah to attend three annual pilgrimage festivals in Jerusalem: Passover, Pentecost, and Tabernacles (Exod 23:14-17). Families often traveled together in large extended-village groups for safety, mutual support, and the social dimension of the journey. The pilgrimage was not merely a religious duty — it was a collective identity event, a yearly reconfirmation of belonging to the people of the Exodus.\f*
\v 42 When Jesus was twelve, they went as usual.
\v 43 \add The journey was made in company — neighbors and relatives from Nazareth, the whole village moving together as they always did.\add* When the festival ended, the group started home. Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem, and his parents did not know.
\v 44 \add They assumed he was somewhere in the caravan — with relatives, with friends, with someone.\add* It was a full day's journey before they looked for him among relatives and acquaintances, and he was not found.
\v 45 They turned back to Jerusalem, searching for him.
\v 46 After three days they found him \add — one day traveling without him, one day returning, and a day of searching the city.\add* He was in the Temple, sitting in the middle of the teachers, listening to them and asking questions.
\v 47 Everyone who heard him was astonished at his understanding and his answers.
\p
\v 48 When his parents saw him, they were astonished. His mother said: “Child, why have you done this to us? Your father and I have been frantic — searching for you in anguish.”
\p
\v 49 He said: “Why were you searching for me? Didn't you know I had to\f + \fr 2:49 \fk TEXT \fq I had to (δεῖ) \ft The Greek dei (δεῖ, “it is necessary,” “I must”) is Jesus's first use of his mission word in Luke's Gospel. The same word marks key moments throughout Luke-Acts: “the Son of Man must suffer” (9:22), “I must proclaim the good news of the kingdom of God” (4:43), and his final words before the crucifixion. It is the language of divine necessity, not personal preference. This is where it begins — at age twelve, in the Temple.\f* be in my Father's house?”\f + \fr 2:49 \fk TEXT \fq my Father's house (ἐν τοῖς τοῦ πατρός μου) \ft The Greek phrase is intentionally ambiguous: “in my Father's house” (spatial — the Temple as God's dwelling) and “about my Father's affairs/business” (purposive — occupied with God's concerns) are both grammatically valid. The ambiguity may be intentional: Jesus identifies God as his Father for the first time in the Gospel, without fully clarifying what that means. Either reading places God — not Joseph — as the one to whom Jesus's primary accountability belongs. The parents do not understand (v. 50), and Luke does not resolve this for them.\f*
\v 50 They did not understand what he meant.
\p
\v 51 He went back down with them to Nazareth and was obedient to them. His mother kept all of it in her heart.\f + \fr 2:51 \fk LEBEN \fq kept all of it in her heart \ft This is the second time Luke uses this phrase (cf. v. 19). The repetition is significant: Mary's posture throughout the chapter is one of guarding and pondering rather than explaining. She cannot explain the shepherds' message, cannot explain the sword Simeon announced, and now cannot explain what her twelve-year-old meant. She keeps it. Spiritual traditions across Christianity (contemplative, charismatic, Reformed) have found in this repeated refrain a model for faith that holds unresolved mystery without forcing premature closure.\f*
\v 52 And Jesus grew — in wisdom and in stature, and in favor with God and with people.
